# Aston Martin 2-litre sports, 1936-1939.
#
# The 2-litre: the larger-engined development of the Ulster line, with the
# long bonnet, the cycle wings and the exposed radiator. The two-seat sports
# body is modelled.
#
# Dimensions: NO reliable published figure was found. The model measures
# 4.08 x 1.58 x 1.28 m on a 2.61 m wheelbase, ESTIMATED from the photographs
# and assumed tyre outside diameters; these are not specifications and must
# not be quoted as such. This is the Speed Model, not the standard tourer.
#
# Shape reference: a grey car with dark blue wings, race number 29, at Pebble
# Beach, in true level side elevation, front three-quarter and rear
# three-quarter.
#
# Colours: grade (d). No British colour list exists; the car was finished to
# order. The objects carry plain names (green, black, red, silver).
#
# Speed 145 km/h, an estimate. Comfort 32/378 on the line 330 + 1.5 x 32:
# an open two-seater with a little more room than the Ulster. Classes 2,3,4.
# Dates are road presence: 1936 intro, retires staggered 1955-1958.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/aston-2litre.blend. Interchange:
# citycars/4.5/aston-2litre.dae. References:
# citycars/references/aston-2litre/ (gitignored; Wikimedia Commons).
#
# Accepted deviations: the wing-to-running-board transition is too abrupt,
# the rear deck and cockpit trim are simplified, and the race number and
# plate lettering are absent. At 128 px the long bonnet, low two-seat body,
# upright Type A radiator and tail-mounted spare read as a 2-litre.


obj=citycar
name=Aston2Litre(Green)
copyright=JamesPetts

DistributionWeight=3
intro_year=1936
intro_month=1
retire_year=1958
retire_month=6
speed=145
class=2,3,4
maximum_comfortable_journey_minutes=32
maximum_tolerable_journey_minutes=378

